need one, and let your gaze rest slightly off the galaxy\u2019s position.\n\nIn binoculars, M100 may be difficult or invisible under ordinary skies. A telescope makes a better invitation: begin at low power to establish the surrounding star field, then increase magnification only if the view remains bright and steady. Under dark skies, look for a faint oval glow with a more concentrated center. Don\u2019t expect a long-exposure photograph\u2019s vivid, curled arms in the eyepiece. Those images gather light over time and process contrast; your eye is working in real time, with a tiny pupil and a moving atmosphere. Averted vision can help, but patience matters just as much.\n\nIf the galaxy doesn\u2019t appear immediately, that is not a failed observation. Check the field, pause, and try again. Sometimes the reward is not a crisp spiral but the recognition of a small, persistent haze\u2014light from a whole galaxy registering at the threshold of sight. M100\u2019s catalogued size and spiral classification give that haze structure on paper. At the eyepiece, the structure is a question: how much can the eye make of light that has travelled so far?\n\n**Source:** [SIMBAD Astronomical Database: NGC 4321 (M100)](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=NGC%204321)"
